---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';

const usage = `<cc-text-input label="Work email" required></cc-text-input>
<cc-button>Create account</cc-button>`;

const iconUsage = `<cc-button>
  <span slot="leading" class="material-icons-round" aria-hidden="true">add</span>
  New project
</cc-button>`;

const sections = [
  ['install', 'Install'],
  ['fonts', 'Fonts are included'],
  ['import', 'Import components'],
  ['use', 'Use them'],
  ['frameworks', 'Frameworks'],
  ['icons', 'Icons'],
];
---

<Base title="Getting started">
  <div class="docs">
    <nav class="side" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="side-heading">On this page</p>
      <ul>{sections.map(([id, label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}</ul>
    </nav>

    <main id="main" class="prose">
      <h1>Getting started</h1>
      <p class="lede">From an empty project to your first component in about two minutes. You need a bundler such as Vite, webpack or the one your framework ships with.</p>

      <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
      <p>The package brings its tokens and <code>lit</code> along as dependencies.</p>
      <Code code="npm i @codechem/components" />

      <h2 id="fonts">Fonts are included</h2>
      <p>The components bundle Kumbh Sans for text and Material Icons Round for icons. Importing a component registers both fonts, so there is nothing to load.</p>

      <h2 id="import">Import components</h2>
      <p>Importing registers the custom elements and adds the design tokens to the page once. Import everything:</p>
      <Code code="import '@codechem/components';" />
      <p>Or only what you use, to keep the bundle small. Every component has its own entry point, listed on the <a href="/components/">Components</a> page.</p>
      <Code code={`import '@codechem/components/button.js';\nimport '@codechem/components/text-input.js';`} />

      <h2 id="use">Use them</h2>
      <p>Write the tags in your markup like any HTML element. Attributes configure them, and they fire standard DOM events such as <code>change</code> and <code>input</code>.</p>
      <Code code={usage} />
      <div class="stage stage-demo">
        <cc-text-input label="Work email" required></cc-text-input>
        <cc-button>Create account</cc-button>
      </div>

      <h2 id="frameworks">Frameworks</h2>
      <dl class="frameworks">
        <dt>React 19+</dt>
        <dd>Works with no setup. React passes attributes and properties to custom elements natively.</dd>
        <dt>Vue</dt>
        <dd>Tell the compiler that <code>cc-</code> tags are custom elements: <code>isCustomElement: (tag) =&gt; tag.startsWith('cc-')</code>.</dd>
        <dt>Angular</dt>
        <dd>Add <code>CUSTOM_ELEMENTS_SCHEMA</code> to the component or module that uses them.</dd>
      </dl>

      <h2 id="icons">Icons</h2>
      <p>Put a Material Icons Round ligature in a component's icon slot. Keep <code>aria-hidden</code> so screen readers skip the icon's name.</p>
      <Code code={iconUsage} />

      <nav class="next" aria-label="Next steps">
        <a href="/components/"><span>Next</span>Browse the components</a>
        <a href="/theming/"><span>Then</span>Make it your brand</a>
      </nav>
    </main>
  </div>
</Base>
